Stop memorizing operator names. Follow what happens to each value instead: once you can read one
value through a pipe, every operator is just a rule about what it does to that value.

## One result, or a stream of values

A **Promise** settles once: one fulfillment value or one rejection, at most. `new Promise(executor)`
runs the executor immediately, and its `.then()` callbacks always run asynchronously. It has no
built-in cancellation method.

An **Observable** delivers zero to many values, and may complete or error. A cold source starts a
new execution for each subscription; a hot source can share a producer that is already running.
Emissions can be synchronous or asynchronous.

`unsubscribe()` stops your subscription, not necessarily the source.

| | Promise | Observable |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Values | One, or a rejection | Zero to many |
| Starts | Immediately, on creation | On subscribe (cold) or already running (hot) |
| Timing | Callbacks always asynchronous | Synchronous or asynchronous |
| Cancel | No built-in method | `unsubscribe()` stops your subscription |

## Reading the diagram

Time moves from left to right. Each dot is a value; each line is a stream.

![A stream diagram: the values A and B on a line that runs from earlier to later, then a vertical bar for completion.](../../../assets/notes/rxjs-fundamentals/reading-a-diagram.webp "Two values, then completion.")

- `next(A)`: a value is emitted.
- `|` complete: the stream ends normally.
- `X` error: the stream ends with a failure.

No more values arrive after complete or error. These lines are not execution threads: they only
show when values happen.

## pipe() composes, subscribe() connects

```ts
const doubled$ = source$.pipe(map((n) => n * 2));

doubled$.subscribe(console.log);
```

`pipe()` builds a new Observable from `source$` and its operators; nothing runs yet. `subscribe()`
connects a consumer and starts the work. With Angular's `HttpClient`, each subscription sends a
request. Angular's `async` pipe can manage the subscription for you.

## map changes the value, filter decides what passes

![Marble diagram: map(n => n * 10) turns 1, 2 and 3 into 10, 20 and 30.](../../../assets/notes/rxjs-fundamentals/map.webp "map changes every value.")

![Marble diagram: filter(n => n >= 20) receives 10, 20 and 30 and lets only 20 and 30 through.](../../../assets/notes/rxjs-fundamentals/filter.webp "filter drops the values that fail the test.")

`filter` drops emissions; it does not emit `true` or `false`.

## tap observes without transforming

![Marble diagram: 20 and 30 enter tap, the console logs 20 and then 30, and the same 20 and 30 come out unchanged.](../../../assets/notes/rxjs-fundamentals/tap.webp "tap logs each value; the stream carries on unchanged.")

Returning a value from `tap` does not change the emitted value. Use it to observe; avoid mutating
objects inside it.

## Putting it together

Follow one value and read the whole pipe:

```ts
source$
  .pipe(
    map((n) => n * 10),
    filter((n) => n >= 20),
    tap((n) => console.log(n)),
  )
  .subscribe(render);
```

| Input | After map | Output |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1, 2, 3 | 10, 20, 30 | 20, 30 |

`2` becomes `20`. It passes the filter. `tap` logs `20`, and the subscriber receives `20`.

**map** transforms, **filter** selects, **tap** observes.

Sources: the RxJS documentation on Observables and operators, and Angular's `HttpClient` guide.
